01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:03.930
And they offered us a certain
amount of money in stock.

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:04.950
It sounded really good.

01:05:04.950 --> 01:05:07.650
More money than I thought that, you know,

01:05:07.650 --> 01:05:10.260
I would ever see in my life before that.

01:05:10.260 --> 01:05:12.210
So I was like, "That sounds great."

01:05:12.210 --> 01:05:14.430
Obviously, this company's
eventually gonna go public.

01:05:14.430 --> 01:05:15.450
That's how they work, right?

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:17.970
I did not know, my lawyer did not tell me

01:05:17.970 --> 01:05:19.140
that that's not always the case.

01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:21.090
The people that we had surrounding us

01:05:21.090 --> 01:05:24.030
that were authority
figures in some way to us,

01:05:24.030 --> 01:05:26.930
on the situation did not
break down the details

01:05:26.930 --> 01:05:28.770
of how that doesn't always happen

01:05:28.770 --> 01:05:30.639
and how that's actually a huge risk.

01:05:30.639 --> 01:05:32.010
- Yeah, yeah, of course.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:05:32.010 --> 01:05:37.010
- So they bought full ownership
and they paid us in yearly,

01:05:37.470 --> 01:05:39.300
you know, annual salaries.

01:05:39.300 --> 01:05:41.462
And they had this structure
set up where it was like,

01:05:41.462 --> 01:05:43.620
this year, eh, it's a
pretty shitty salary,

01:05:43.620 --> 01:05:45.930
but next year, it's an okay salary.

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:48.870
But by year four, you're
gonna love your salary.

01:05:48.870 --> 01:05:49.860
So-
- Did that happen?

01:05:49.860 --> 01:05:52.197
- It did eventually play out that way.

01:05:52.197 --> 01:05:54.430
And I was pretty comfortable with it.

01:05:54.430 --> 01:05:58.530
I was like, we are making
a good salary here.

01:05:58.530 --> 01:06:00.990
Like, what do we really
have to complain about?

01:06:00.990 --> 01:06:02.760
- And I guess they're
doing a lot of the stuff

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:03.900
that you used to have to do

01:06:03.900 --> 01:06:05.790
and they're looking for
things and managing it.

01:06:05.790 --> 01:06:07.410
But then also I imagine as a YouTuber,

01:06:07.410 --> 01:06:10.180
it's probably quite difficult
to give that control

01:06:10.180 --> 01:06:11.760
to someone else.
- It was, because I did

01:06:11.760 --> 01:06:12.930
give that control away

01:06:12.930 --> 01:06:14.970
and everything was not
how I woulda done it.

01:06:14.970 --> 01:06:18.240
So I ended up working
double time to teach them

01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:20.970
how to do the things, but
then they were rehiring.

01:06:20.970 --> 01:06:22.590
And the way that bigger companies work

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:23.970
is just like, you're never just building

01:06:23.970 --> 01:06:25.350
a one-on-one relationship with someone

01:06:25.350 --> 01:06:27.240
who like you teach 'em once and they know,

01:06:27.240 --> 01:06:28.920
and they keep going with that thing.

01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:30.270
And then it's just a
thing that happens now.

01:06:30.270 --> 01:06:31.651
It's like, no, you're reteaching

01:06:31.651 --> 01:06:33.720
and reteaching and
reteaching until it's like,

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:35.740
okay, let's abandon the website.

01:06:35.740 --> 01:06:37.740
And then it was like me
abandoning a lot of the things

01:06:37.740 --> 01:06:39.840
that I had a dream of growing

01:06:39.840 --> 01:06:42.120
because I was not able to keep that up

01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:43.890
and also do the videos.

01:06:43.890 --> 01:06:46.008
So, you know, I had to figure
out where to put my time.

01:06:46.008 --> 01:06:47.640
- Yeah, was there a specific point

01:06:47.640 --> 01:06:49.320
when you kind of realized, damn,

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:52.260
this is not what I envisioned
at all and I kinda want out?

01:06:52.260 --> 01:06:54.030
Like, was there a specific moment

01:06:54.030 --> 01:06:56.430
or was it just like a build
up of a lot of things?

01:06:56.430 --> 01:07:00.660
- It was primarily a buildup
over a long period of time.

01:07:00.660 --> 01:07:03.910
It was about four years in when I realized

01:07:04.800 --> 01:07:06.780
that things just weren't looking right.

01:07:06.780 --> 01:07:09.330
And I still had two years
on the contract left.

01:07:09.330 --> 01:07:12.570
And I think one particular thing was like,

01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:15.900
they hired someone that I did not know

01:07:15.900 --> 01:07:16.770
that they were gonna hire.

01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:17.910
And then all of a sudden I found out

01:07:17.910 --> 01:07:19.500
that they were now a full-time employee

01:07:19.500 --> 01:07:21.360
in this really important position

01:07:21.360 --> 01:07:22.193
that meant something to me.

01:07:22.193 --> 01:07:23.790
And I was like, "Wait, I didn't realize

01:07:23.790 --> 01:07:25.350
that was what was gonna happen."

01:07:25.350 --> 01:07:27.450
And also, there were times,

01:07:27.450 --> 01:07:29.340
and I don't know how much I could say,

01:07:29.340 --> 01:07:30.516
but I'll see what I can do.

01:07:30.516 --> 01:07:31.633
(men laugh)
- Well, they don't exist

01:07:31.633 --> 01:07:34.323
anymore, right, so.
- True, technically.

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:40.213
There was a fundraiser that they put on.

01:07:40.213 --> 01:07:42.750
- Oh.
- It was for a game.

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:45.540
And they had just seen the
success of other people

01:07:45.540 --> 01:07:47.280
who had done fundraisers for games,

01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:48.113
Indiegogo,
- Oh, you're right.

01:07:48.113 --> 01:07:50.040
- GoFundMe type things.

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:54.360
And they raised like
$250,000 for these games,

01:07:54.360 --> 01:07:55.980
which were like mobile games,

01:07:55.980 --> 01:07:56.970
but you could play it on the computer.

01:07:56.970 --> 01:07:58.110
And it was like this really cool thing

01:07:58.110 --> 01:08:00.600
with all these inside
jokes from the community.

01:08:00.600 --> 01:08:03.540
And they said, "Oh, that's
cool, guy, you do that."

01:08:03.540 --> 01:08:06.090
And then we were like, "We don't
have a concept for a game."

01:08:06.090 --> 01:08:07.290
They're like, "Ah, we'll
figure it out later.

01:08:07.290 --> 01:08:09.240
You just raise some money."
- Oh, what, so they

01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:10.860
just made the fundraiser.

01:08:10.860 --> 01:08:13.110
And then afterwards, they
were like, "Make the game."

01:08:13.110 --> 01:08:15.930
- And we said, "We need to have something

01:08:15.930 --> 01:08:17.460
to pitch to people, we need."

01:08:17.460 --> 01:08:18.330
And then they were like, "Cool, well,

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:19.467
just tell 'em the idea then."

01:08:19.467 --> 01:08:21.000
And then they were like,
"You come up with the idea."

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:23.310
So I came up with the
idea, Ian, of course, also.

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:24.750
We both came up with the idea.

01:08:24.750 --> 01:08:29.670
And they were like, "Cool, you
got the idea, let's do it."

01:08:29.670 --> 01:08:31.410
And I was like, "We need
to have concept art,

01:08:31.410 --> 01:08:33.750
we need to like show a build of the game.

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:34.770
We need to be able to show

01:08:34.770 --> 01:08:36.244
what we want to do with this thing."

01:08:36.244 --> 01:08:37.890
And they were like,
"Ah, we don't have time.

01:08:37.890 --> 01:08:39.240
Okay, we'll get concept art."

01:08:39.240 --> 01:08:42.360
So all we were able to do was
launch with this concept art.

01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:43.362
- Wow.
- Oh my God.

01:08:43.362 --> 01:08:47.640
- And launching a campaign, asking people,

01:08:47.640 --> 01:08:50.430
incentivizing your fans to give you money,

01:08:50.430 --> 01:08:52.050
well, it did not feel right to me,

01:08:52.050 --> 01:08:53.670
but I felt backed up into a corner.

01:08:53.670 --> 01:08:57.270
And, you know, I think that's
probably one of the things

01:08:57.270 --> 01:08:59.610
that I regret from my time there,

01:08:59.610 --> 01:09:02.730
is allowing myself to feel
backed up into a corner,

01:09:02.730 --> 01:09:04.890
to accept money from
people that were excited

01:09:04.890 --> 01:09:06.240
about this thing being built.

01:09:06.240 --> 01:09:08.160
Of course, you know, we
did accept money for that.

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:10.350
And I did put all of my heart and soul

01:09:10.350 --> 01:09:12.510
into making it as good
as it possibly could be,

01:09:12.510 --> 01:09:14.310
still didn't live up to my expectations.

01:09:14.310 --> 01:09:16.230
And I feel like if it was done
the right way, it would've.

01:09:16.230 --> 01:09:19.230
But the moment that really stood out to me

01:09:19.230 --> 01:09:22.890
was there was some
shadiness about, you know,

01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:24.690
obviously the way that
they pushed to earn money

01:09:24.690 --> 01:09:26.640
for something because
they saw dollar signs.

01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:28.500
- Right, not because it was good projects.

01:09:28.500 --> 01:09:30.900
- Rather than it being
like, those types of things,

01:09:30.900 --> 01:09:33.030
if you're gonna have
your audience fund them,

01:09:33.030 --> 01:09:34.980
it should really be a passion project.

01:09:34.980 --> 01:09:35.850
- Absolutely.
- Exactly.

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:37.350
- And I could have made
it a passion project

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:38.790
'cause I really was excited about it,

01:09:38.790 --> 01:09:42.600
but it was so forced and rushed.

01:09:42.600 --> 01:09:47.600
And then there was some shady stuff

01:09:47.640 --> 01:09:50.250
about like the way that they contributed

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:53.340
to certain donations to inflate

01:09:53.340 --> 01:09:54.300
- Huh.
- the way that

01:09:54.300 --> 01:09:55.133
certain things looked.

01:09:55.133 --> 01:09:56.400
And that's really all I'll say about that,

01:09:56.400 --> 01:09:57.840
but it made me feel so weird about it.

01:09:57.840 --> 01:10:00.810
I was like, "I need out
as soon as possible."

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:02.670
- I imagine when they were
doing stuff like that,

01:10:02.670 --> 01:10:05.340
the confidence you had in the company

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:07.650
was probably a bit like,
okay, if they're willing

01:10:07.650 --> 01:10:09.880
to do this, stuff must
not be going so great.

01:10:09.880 --> 01:10:14.160
- And I realized that the
way that they treated that

01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:16.380
was the same way that
they were treating me

01:10:16.380 --> 01:10:17.670
and all the other people.

01:10:17.670 --> 01:10:19.418
- Because a lotta people
lost a lot of money, right,

01:10:19.418 --> 01:10:20.949
I mean, it was like crazy.

01:10:20.949 --> 01:10:22.573
- What was it?
- A lot of people lost

01:10:22.573 --> 01:10:24.480
a lot of money on YouTube.
- Oh, yeah, yeah, eventually.

01:10:24.480 --> 01:10:27.270
Luckily, I wouldn't
say I foresaw anything,

01:10:27.270 --> 01:10:28.103
but it didn't feel right.

01:10:28.103 --> 01:10:30.647
And I got out a year before
they ended up closing down.

01:10:31.710 --> 01:10:34.980
And another thing that
really was a turning point

01:10:34.980 --> 01:10:39.980
was I had made a Facebook
page and it was, I don't know,

01:10:40.500 --> 01:10:43.500
I had like 500, 600,000 followers

01:10:43.500 --> 01:10:45.090
or whatever you call it on Facebook.

01:10:45.090 --> 01:10:46.488
- It's just like Anthony.
- Subscribers.

01:10:46.488 --> 01:10:47.977
- Friends.
- Friends.

01:10:47.977 --> 01:10:50.250
- Friends, friends, yes.
(men laugh)

01:10:50.250 --> 01:10:53.130
And, yeah, it was "Smosh" Anthony.

01:10:53.130 --> 01:10:55.680
And they were like, "Oh,
this has "Smosh" in the name,

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:58.890
so we're gonna run this for you, okay?"

01:10:58.890 --> 01:11:01.020
I was like, "Okay, yeah, yeah, sure."

01:11:01.020 --> 01:11:02.500
And then I was like, hey, you know,

01:11:02.500 --> 01:11:05.700
I was kinda trying to start
to silently transition out.

01:11:05.700 --> 01:11:07.230
I think they kinda picked up on the gist

01:11:07.230 --> 01:11:09.360
'cause we still were
contractually obligated.

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:12.690
But I was like, "Can I
get my Facebook back?"

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:14.550
They're like, "No, it's ours."

01:11:14.550 --> 01:11:15.753
I was like-
- Oh.

01:11:15.753 --> 01:11:17.130
- Oh!
- "But I made that thing.

01:11:17.130 --> 01:11:18.960
And you said that you
just wanted to run it

01:11:18.960 --> 01:11:20.490
because you were gonna help
me post content on there."

01:11:20.490 --> 01:11:22.620
They're like, "It has "Smosh" in the name,

01:11:22.620 --> 01:11:25.260
this is ours."
- (gasps) That's fucked up.

01:11:25.260 --> 01:11:27.873
- I was like, "Okay."
- Geez.

01:11:29.220 --> 01:11:31.097
- And then I easily settled on that.

01:11:31.097 --> 01:11:32.640
I was like, "Oh, okay, it's fine.

01:11:32.640 --> 01:11:34.740
It's just Facebook, who
cares, it's Facebook."

01:11:34.740 --> 01:11:37.357
And then they were like,

01:11:37.357 --> 01:11:40.200
"Okay, hey, can we get
access to your Twitter

01:11:40.200 --> 01:11:41.595
and Instagram?"
(Connor inhales sharply)

01:11:41.595 --> 01:11:43.743
I was like, "No."
- (laughs) No.

01:11:44.901 --> 01:11:46.710
- What the fuck?
- And then I immediately

01:11:46.710 --> 01:11:49.182
changed the name from "Smosh"
Anthony to Anthony Padilla

01:11:49.182 --> 01:11:50.510
(hosts laugh)

01:11:50.510 --> 01:11:52.860
- Not on my watch!
- after they asked me

01:11:52.860 --> 01:11:53.730
for access to that.

01:11:53.730 --> 01:11:58.020
- In a way, it's kind of cool
that they kind of forced you

01:11:58.020 --> 01:11:58.853
into being like, "You know what?

01:11:58.853 --> 01:11:59.686
Fuck this, I'm just gonna go on my own.

01:11:59.686 --> 01:12:01.782
Like, I'm gonna fucking brand myself.

01:12:01.782 --> 01:12:03.510
Fuck these guys."
- It really got me thinking

01:12:03.510 --> 01:12:05.180
like, could I do something
- Could I do this?

01:12:05.180 --> 01:12:09.480
- on my own outside of this
brand that I had created?

01:12:09.480 --> 01:12:12.483
Yeah, and it was really sad
because I had made that brand,

01:12:13.323 --> 01:12:15.780
- "Smosh" was yours, yeah.
- when I was 14 years old,

01:12:15.780 --> 01:12:18.150
I made that as like a
little shitty website,

01:12:18.150 --> 01:12:21.300
as like a forum for me and
my group of friends at school

01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:23.910
to go on and hang out with each other.

01:12:23.910 --> 01:12:25.170
It was really before social media,

01:12:25.170 --> 01:12:26.940
it was like, we were either talking on AIM

01:12:26.940 --> 01:12:29.190
or we were going onto this website.

01:12:29.190 --> 01:12:32.704
And, yeah, it was sad to
kinda let that part of me,

01:12:32.704 --> 01:12:36.630
this baby that I had brought
into this world, kinda go.

01:12:36.630 --> 01:12:38.280
- Yeah, God, it must be nerve-racking

01:12:38.280 --> 01:12:39.400
trying to do something
yourself though, right?

01:12:39.400 --> 01:12:41.730
- Yeah.
- Because as a YouTuber

01:12:41.730 --> 01:12:44.190
starting, ditching your channel,

01:12:44.190 --> 01:12:46.293
which has like millions and
millions of subscribers,

01:12:46.293 --> 01:12:48.960
to start from the ground up again.

01:12:48.960 --> 01:12:51.870
I think like you were one
of the first people I know,

01:12:51.870 --> 01:12:54.480
like the first big creators,
to really like branch off

01:12:54.480 --> 01:12:56.130
and try to do something themselves

01:12:56.130 --> 01:12:57.729
with a new channel, right?

01:12:57.729 --> 01:12:58.829
- And it succeeded.
- And doing well.

01:12:58.829 --> 01:13:01.740
- And there wasn't a precedent
set before that really

01:13:01.740 --> 01:13:04.090
that you could really succeed
by doing something new, right?

01:13:04.090 --> 01:13:05.577
- Right, I mean, well, Fred did it.

01:13:05.577 --> 01:13:06.600
- Oh.
- Oh, yeah.

01:13:06.600 --> 01:13:08.340
- Fred had another channel

01:13:08.340 --> 01:13:09.590
and then he did his thing.
- That's true, that's true.

01:13:09.590 --> 01:13:10.559
- Well, he did a few movies.

01:13:10.559 --> 01:13:12.313
You had a few movies too.

01:13:12.313 --> 01:13:13.439
- I did a movie.

01:13:13.439 --> 01:13:15.061
- How was doing a movie?

01:13:15.061 --> 01:13:16.080
- How was doing a movie,

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:18.420
ah, man.
(hosts laugh)

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:20.430
That was also another weird experience.

01:13:20.430 --> 01:13:23.267
- But a part of me, 'cause,
obviously, I mean, do you,

01:13:23.267 --> 01:13:25.548
(laughs) it's not a good movie.

01:13:25.548 --> 01:13:27.208
(men laugh)

01:13:27.208 --> 01:13:28.396
I was just, ah, fuck it,

01:13:28.396 --> 01:13:29.728
I was trying to think of how to say it.

01:13:29.728 --> 01:13:30.793
It's not a good movie.

01:13:30.793 --> 01:13:33.060
But it's-
- My heart, no, no.

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:34.487
I put my heart and soul into that movie,

01:13:34.487 --> 01:13:35.857
it's the best movie.
- Wow, right in front of him?

01:13:35.857 --> 01:13:37.901
- Oh my God.
- I feel like-

01:13:37.901 --> 01:13:39.051
- This is up fucked up.

01:13:40.650 --> 01:13:42.300
- It's a part of you-
- We invite him onto the show.

01:13:42.300 --> 01:13:45.450
- You invite me on here to talk
shit about my pride and joy?

01:13:45.450 --> 01:13:47.144
- Listen, I wanna talk to you,

01:13:47.144 --> 01:13:48.670
answer me then.
- Did you actually watch that?

01:13:48.670 --> 01:13:50.610
- Yeah, I loved it.
- Dear lord, wait,

01:13:50.610 --> 01:13:51.463
- I love it in a way of like-
- (laughs) you said

01:13:51.463 --> 01:13:54.293
you loved it?
- Yeah, it's a terrible movie.

01:13:54.293 --> 01:13:55.997
(men laugh)
- It's so fucking bad.

01:13:55.997 --> 01:13:57.150
- It's like it's so bad that it's good.

01:13:57.150 --> 01:13:59.250
- It's so fucking bad.
- Yeah, but it's that amazing

01:13:59.250 --> 01:14:00.720
part of YouTube history

01:14:00.720 --> 01:14:02.790
where like everyone had to have a movie.

01:14:02.790 --> 01:14:04.463
YouTube for some reason was like,

01:14:04.463 --> 01:14:06.810
"Make a fucking movie, pop them out."

01:14:06.810 --> 01:14:08.889
Fred had fucking John Cena in his movie.

01:14:08.889 --> 01:14:11.097
(all chattering)
- Oh, yeah. (laughs)

01:14:11.097 --> 01:14:12.147
- I completely forgot about that.

01:14:12.147 --> 01:14:14.730
- Dude.
- It was just a fucking cool,

01:14:14.730 --> 01:14:18.990
weird time where people were
making movies, whereas like,

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:20.760
I mean, did you even wanna make a movie?

01:14:20.760 --> 01:14:22.530
Was it like, what was that,

01:14:22.530 --> 01:14:23.627
what was going on with that?

01:14:23.627 --> 01:14:25.920
'Cause I feel like there
must have been so many people

01:14:25.920 --> 01:14:26.820
trying to put their things in there,

01:14:26.820 --> 01:14:28.500
trying to be like, "No, it should be this,

01:14:28.500 --> 01:14:29.333
it should be this."

01:14:29.333 --> 01:14:31.207
"No, no, no, fuck, well,
